<style>
  /*
   * Eight of these stack down the chapter, so their separation is paid seven
   * times. It was 3.5rem of collapsed margin plus 2.5rem of padding above the
   * rule — 126px between one comparison ending and the next one being named,
   * measured, and the single largest repeated gap in the document. The rule
   * already does the separating; the space around it only has to keep the rule
   * from touching the text.
   */
  .mistake {
    margin: 2.25rem 0;
    padding-top: 1.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
  }
  /*
   * The numbered title. `>` and `h2`, both load-bearing.
   *
   * This was `.mistake h3` from when the titles were `h3`s. They moved up a
   * level and the rule did not, so the only thing left matching it was the two
   * `h3`s inside the cards at the end of the chapter — which then rendered at
   * `--text-xl`, the same 33.84px as the `h2` above them, and a card heading
   * claimed the rank of a section heading. A descendant selector for a direct
   * child is how that happens; this one cannot reach into a card.
   *
   * No `font-size`: `h2` is already `--text-xl` and saying it twice is how the
   * two got out of step in the first place.
   */
  .mistake > h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.8rem;
  }
  .num {
    font-family: var(--font-numeric);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .two-up {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* The figure track has to hold an 821px chart card before the layout is
     worth splitting; below this it stacks and the chart takes the full
     column instead of scrolling inside a track too narrow for it. */
  @media (min-width: 78rem) {
    .two-up {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr minmax(200px, 260px);
      /*
       * Centred, the same bargain the hero makes with its map.
       *
       * The aside is one figure and a line of text; the plot beside it is
       * several hundred pixels tall. Stretched, the column's content sat at the
       * top and left a measured 260 x 364px of nothing under it, which reads as
       * a column that stopped early. Centred, the same emptiness splits above
       * and below and reads as an annotation standing beside a figure.
       */
      align-items: center;
    }
  }
  .two-up figure {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .verdict .stat {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  /*
   * The two cards line up because the number is anchored to the bottom of an
   * equal-height card, not because their tags wrapped the same way.
   *
   * They were flex items on `align-items: center`, and 「含 PM10（當年的做法）」
   * wraps while 「不含 PM10」 does not — so measured at 1000 the cards were 256px
   * and 220px tall with tops 18px apart, and 0.772 and 0.524, the two numbers a
   * reader is asked to compare, sat on different lines.
   *
   * `subgrid` is the right tool for this in principle and was tried twice. It
   * does not take here — measured, the card's rows still resolved to four fixed
   * sizes and the values stayed 25 to 49px apart at 500, 640, 768 and 1000,
   * every width where the two tags wrap to different line counts. A rule that
   * looks correct and does nothing is worse than a cruder one that is checkable.
   *
   * `align-items: stretch` makes the cards the same height; `margin-top: auto`
   * on the value pushes it, the label and the note — three single lines in both
   * cards — to a shared bottom, whatever the tag above them did.
   */
  .compare {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0;
  }
  .compare .card {
    flex: 1 1 190px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2rem;
  }
  /*
   * Two rows vary independently, so both are pinned.
   *
   * Measured, the two cards differ in two places and not the same one each time:
   * in block 02 the TAG wraps (「含 PM10（當年的做法）」 two lines against 「不含
   * PM10」 one), and in block 07 the NOTE does (「只答 61%，其餘拒答」 65px against
   * 「答了 70%」 32px). Bottom-anchoring alone fixed the first and broke on the
   * second; a fixed tag alone would do the reverse.
   *
   * So the tag always occupies two lines — `lh` is exactly one line box, so this
   * needs no guess about the type size — and the note is pushed to the bottom of
   * an equal-height card. What is left between them is the value and its label,
   * one line each, starting at the same offset in both cards.
   */
  .compare .tag {
    min-height: 2lh;
  }
  .compare .stat-note {
    margin-top: auto;
  }

  .arrow {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  .arrow {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    flex: none;
    align-self: center;
